      {"question": "Ammonia or CO2 for a Nebraska cold storage facility?", "answer": "Humid continental — cold winters aid heat rejection For large freezer warehouses, ammonia (R-717) usually wins on efficiency but brings IIAR/ASHRAE 15 machinery-room requirements and process safety obligations. CO2 (R-744) transcritical systems simplify permitting and suit cooler or mid-size applications. We model both against the facility's climate, size, and operating profile and recommend the system the numbers support."},
      {"question": "Can you convert an existing warehouse to cold storage in Nebraska?", "answer": "Often — but four questions decide it: can the slab take a heated sub-slab or ventilated underfloor against frost heave, can the roof carry new refrigeration equipment, does the electrical service have capacity for compressor loads, and can the envelope accept a continuous vapor retarder. We answer those with engineering, not optimism, before recommending conversion over greenfield."},
      {"question": "Is Apex Grid licensed to seal drawings in Nebraska?", "answer": "Yes. Apex Grid Engineering is licensed in Nebraska and seals MEP and structural drawings through a licensed engineer of record qualified in the state. Every seal follows responsible-charge review — the engineer defines the scope, verifies the design basis, and takes professional responsibility for the sealed documents."}
