      {"heading": "Building envelope and floors for Wisconsin cold storage", "body": "The envelope is a refrigeration component. Insulated metal panels — 2 to 4 inches for coolers, 5 to 6 inches for freezers — carry the thermal load; vapor retarder continuity at every joint and penetration decides whether the assembly stays dry for decades or slowly saturates. Humid continental — cold winters strongly favor refrigeration efficiency Freezer floors get heated sub-slabs or ventilated underfloors engineered against frost heave, because a heaved slab is a structural failure, not a maintenance item. High-speed doors, airlocks, and dock seals choke the infiltration loads that otherwise dominate the refrigeration sizing."},
      {"heading": "Food safety and code compliance in Wisconsin", "body": "FDA's Food Safety Modernization Act requires written preventive-controls plans for food facilities — the building either supports that plan or fights it. We design washable finishes, sloped floors to drains, pest-exclusion detailing, temperature-monitoring infrastructure, and allergen segregation into the drawings. The Port of Milwaukee handles Great Lakes cargo. Wisconsin produces more cheese than any other state — cheddar aging, mozzarella freezing, and dairy distribution need vast cooler and freezer space.  Refrigeration machinery rooms comply with ASHRAE 15 and IIAR standards; ammonia systems above threshold quantities trigger OSHA process safety management. Every sealed sheet carries responsible-charge engineering by a Wisconsin-licensed PE."},
      {"heading": "Wisconsin cold storage markets we serve", "body": "Apex Grid engineers cold storage projects across Wisconsin, with focused coverage in its primary food and logistics markets: I-94 Milwaukee-Madison corridor. Each market below carries jurisdiction-specific engineering notes — the local conditions that shape refrigeration, envelope, and permitting decisions."}
